WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.409 --> 00:00:05.339
- Hello, and welcome to "Ed
Talks with Khan Academy."

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:08.579
I am excited today to
talk with Kara Bobroff,

00:00:08.579 --> 00:00:12.389
who is the founder of the Native
American Community Academy

00:00:12.389 --> 00:00:14.799
and NACA Inspired Schools.

00:00:14.799 --> 00:00:18.439
We're gonna talk about
culture in education broadly

00:00:18.439 --> 00:00:23.329
and the education of Native
American students in particular,

00:00:23.329 --> 00:00:25.279
so looking forward to that.

00:00:25.279 --> 00:00:28.029
Before we get started, a couple of notes.

00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:29.729
A reminder that Khan Academy

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:34.209
is a nonprofit organization,
and we are able to do our work

00:00:34.209 --> 00:00:37.939
thanks to the generous
donations from people like you.

00:00:37.939 --> 00:00:41.689
If you go to khanacademy.org/donate,

00:00:41.689 --> 00:00:46.119
you will see and have the
opportunity to be able to donate

00:00:46.119 --> 00:00:47.982
to help us continue our mission.

00:00:49.329 --> 00:00:52.169
Similarly, we wanna thank
some of our supporters

00:00:52.169 --> 00:00:55.589
who really stepped up
during the COVID crisis

00:00:55.589 --> 00:00:56.546
to help us with our work,

00:00:56.546 --> 00:01:01.459
and that includes Bank of
America, AT&amp;T, Google.org,

00:01:01.459 --> 00:01:05.042
Novartis, Fastly, and General Motors.

00:01:06.939 --> 00:01:11.939
Reminder, if you want
to hear this talk again

00:01:12.239 --> 00:01:14.909
or if you have missed
some and wanna catch up,

00:01:14.909 --> 00:01:18.829
you can get audio versions of these talks

00:01:18.829 --> 00:01:21.859
at the "Homeroom with Sal" podcast,

00:01:21.859 --> 00:01:24.232
available wherever you get your podcasts.

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:29.479
So with that, let me welcome Kara Bobroff.

00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:31.519
It's nice to have you.

00:01:31.519 --> 00:01:32.352
- It's nice to be here.

00:01:32.352 --> 00:01:34.949
I'm super excited for our conversation,

00:01:34.949 --> 00:01:36.799
and I'm really looking forward to it.

00:01:38.209 --> 00:01:42.549
- Great, so I like to
start off asking people,

00:01:42.549 --> 00:01:45.289
how did you get to where you are today?

00:01:45.289 --> 00:01:48.185
What was, tell us a
little bit about your path

00:01:48.185 --> 00:01:50.799
to starting these schools and this network

00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:52.649
and all the work you do.

00:01:52.649 --> 00:01:54.822
- Yeah, thank you, Kristen, for that.

00:01:55.769 --> 00:01:58.549
It's a interesting story
of like two or three

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:01.299
different dynamics of a journey,

00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:03.299
both personal, professional,

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:05.429
and then also like kind of a calling

00:02:05.429 --> 00:02:08.659
or what needed to happen
within our community.

00:02:08.659 --> 00:02:10.884
So I'm Navajo and Lakota.

00:02:10.884 --> 00:02:12.430
I'm gonna introduce myself in that way.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:17.216
(Kara speaking in foreign language)

00:02:17.216 --> 00:02:19.899
So on my mother's side,
biological mother's side,

00:02:19.899 --> 00:02:22.579
I am Navajo, and our family's
from a community in New Mexico

00:02:22.579 --> 00:02:25.469
called Ramah, which is not
contiguous with Navajo Nation

00:02:25.469 --> 00:02:27.309
but are Navajo Nation.

00:02:27.309 --> 00:02:29.149
And on that side, we're the Salt Clan,

00:02:29.149 --> 00:02:30.399
and that's how we identify.

00:02:30.399 --> 00:02:32.799
So we recognize our relatives,

00:02:32.799 --> 00:02:34.389
so if any relatives out there today

00:02:34.389 --> 00:02:37.539
that are also Salt Clan and
Navajo just wanted to say,

00:02:37.539 --> 00:02:41.249
you know, open up kind of
conversation on that level,

00:02:41.249 --> 00:02:43.669
just to say, that's an
important relationship.

00:02:43.669 --> 00:02:46.399
The second part is
Lakota, and on that side,

00:02:46.399 --> 00:02:49.179
my biological father was
from Pine Ridge area,

00:02:49.179 --> 00:02:51.599
and so near Pine Ridge and Rosebud.

00:02:51.599 --> 00:02:54.329
I grew up in Albuquerque, New Mexico,

00:02:54.329 --> 00:02:56.919
and basically my parents
were both educators.

00:02:56.919 --> 00:02:59.219
My mom was a middle school
teacher her entire life.

00:02:59.219 --> 00:03:03.119
My father was a teacher,
assistant principal, principal,

00:03:03.119 --> 00:03:05.679
became a systems leader as
well of our school district,

00:03:05.679 --> 00:03:07.639
but not really knowing I
was gonna become a teacher.

00:03:07.639 --> 00:03:12.639
So really had a important aspect
of something that happened

00:03:13.179 --> 00:03:14.929
when I was about 19 years old.

00:03:14.929 --> 00:03:16.849
I served as a mental health worker

00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:18.349
in a residential treatment center,

00:03:18.349 --> 00:03:20.549
and that was the first time
I started to work with youth

00:03:20.549 --> 00:03:24.639
who were six years, seven years,
eight years younger than I

00:03:24.639 --> 00:03:28.139
who were going in and
out of various systems,

00:03:28.139 --> 00:03:30.779
from foster care to, you
know, the psychiatric hospital

00:03:30.779 --> 00:03:32.909
to home for all sorts of reasons.

00:03:32.909 --> 00:03:36.229
And I think that really
set in stone for me

00:03:36.229 --> 00:03:38.329
that those students had
been, or children had been

00:03:38.329 --> 00:03:39.199
through more in their life

00:03:39.199 --> 00:03:40.749
than I would ever have to really endure

00:03:40.749 --> 00:03:43.909
and kinda like really
drew me into education,

00:03:43.909 --> 00:03:46.089
although my family's
influence had for sure.

00:03:46.089 --> 00:03:48.339
Became a special education
teacher at the school I went to,

00:03:48.339 --> 00:03:50.369
started to see all the good things

00:03:50.369 --> 00:03:52.349
about being a teacher
and also started to see

00:03:52.349 --> 00:03:54.259
some inequities around who had access

00:03:54.259 --> 00:03:56.789
to what kind of courses and
how that was determined,

00:03:56.789 --> 00:03:58.699
predetermined for a lotta kids

00:03:58.699 --> 00:04:00.869
and reflected back on my own experience

00:04:00.869 --> 00:04:03.209
and saw that that was probably
true when I was there.

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:05.103
Just like there was a group of us,

00:04:05.103 --> 00:04:06.929
and 1/3 of us went to one classroom,

00:04:06.929 --> 00:04:09.639
1/3 of us went to another,
and 1/3 of us went to another.

00:04:09.639 --> 00:04:11.399
And I'm not really sure why,

00:04:11.399 --> 00:04:13.379
but then we would kind of like reconnect.

00:04:13.379 --> 00:04:15.372
So that was interesting to experience.

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:17.919
Served as assistant
principal at the school

00:04:17.919 --> 00:04:20.647
my older siblings went to,
which freaked them out. (laughs)

00:04:20.647 --> 00:04:21.480
(Kristen laughs)
They'd come in and like,

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:22.909
"How can you be like
the assistant principal

00:04:22.909 --> 00:04:24.949
where we went to school?"

00:04:24.949 --> 00:04:26.529
My journey took me to the Bay Area,

00:04:26.529 --> 00:04:29.589
and I served as a dean of
students in Marin County

00:04:29.589 --> 00:04:31.829
in school, Miller Creek Middle School,

00:04:31.829 --> 00:04:33.479
and that was the first time

00:04:33.479 --> 00:04:35.459
I started to see a couple things.

00:04:35.459 --> 00:04:38.029
One, that school provided
so many opportunities

00:04:38.029 --> 00:04:39.349
for their students and families

00:04:39.349 --> 00:04:41.589
and was really responsive
to their community.

00:04:41.589 --> 00:04:44.439
It wasn't a question of which
college kids would go to.

00:04:44.439 --> 00:04:46.169
It was a question of like what college,

00:04:46.169 --> 00:04:47.449
I mean, if they would go to college,

00:04:47.449 --> 00:04:49.729
it was a question of like
which college they would go to,

00:04:49.729 --> 00:04:52.339
which was very different,
much more responsive

00:04:52.339 --> 00:04:54.359
socially and emotionally to the students.

00:04:54.359 --> 00:04:56.549
However, there was a lack of diversity.

00:04:56.549 --> 00:04:57.989
So also from an equity standpoint,

00:04:57.989 --> 00:04:59.559
just kinda saw like the difference

00:04:59.559 --> 00:05:02.099
between what students
and teachers had there

00:05:02.099 --> 00:05:05.729
as a public school than what
I kind of experienced myself

00:05:05.729 --> 00:05:07.649
and how schools were
operated at that time.

00:05:07.649 --> 00:05:09.719
Also, during that time back home,

00:05:09.719 --> 00:05:10.959
I was there for four years,

00:05:10.959 --> 00:05:13.746
there was a conversation around
Native American education

00:05:13.746 --> 00:05:15.192
and a need for Native American
magnet or charter school,

00:05:15.192 --> 00:05:18.479
and that's probably the first
time I heard about that,

00:05:18.479 --> 00:05:20.719
and I always thought, "That'd
be a cool place to work

00:05:20.719 --> 00:05:22.849
when I moved back home." (laughs)

00:05:22.849 --> 00:05:26.879
However, you know, I think
that that was kind of grounded

00:05:26.879 --> 00:05:29.429
in the fact that many of our elders

00:05:29.429 --> 00:05:31.829
and those who'd come
before in a urban setting,

00:05:31.829 --> 00:05:33.889
you know, always had a
longing to do something more

00:05:33.889 --> 00:05:36.919
for Native youth in
Albuquerque specifically,

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:39.479
knowing that that district
serves about 80,000 kids,

00:05:39.479 --> 00:05:42.199
6 to 7,000 self-identify as Native.

00:05:42.199 --> 00:05:46.039
And in like that time to now,
when we look at the census,

00:05:46.039 --> 00:05:49.029
that population of urban
Indigenous families

00:05:49.029 --> 00:05:50.899
has increased not only in Albuquerque

00:05:50.899 --> 00:05:52.819
but across the United States.

00:05:52.819 --> 00:05:55.529
So just kind of got an interest in that.

00:05:55.529 --> 00:05:58.849
Would go back and forth
for different, you know,

00:05:58.849 --> 00:06:00.329
times just having that conversation,

00:06:00.329 --> 00:06:02.489
and nothing really kinda came to.

00:06:02.489 --> 00:06:04.449
My journey to Shiprock, New Mexico,

00:06:04.449 --> 00:06:07.999
where I was served as a principal
at Newcomb Middle School,

00:06:07.999 --> 00:06:10.249
which was like the best school on Earth

00:06:10.249 --> 00:06:12.909
in the sense of like a place
I really felt connected.

00:06:12.909 --> 00:06:16.709
I kinda thought, like I didn't
speak my language fluently,

00:06:16.709 --> 00:06:18.589
so I didn't know if I
would be the best principal

00:06:18.589 --> 00:06:21.809
for the community, but what
I found was a couple things.

00:06:21.809 --> 00:06:24.689
One, that the strengths of
the students and families

00:06:24.689 --> 00:06:26.559
and the cultural knowledge was so great

00:06:26.559 --> 00:06:28.769
that it should be
incorporated into the school

00:06:28.769 --> 00:06:31.579
as like how the students
in Marin had so many things

00:06:31.579 --> 00:06:34.499
brought into their school, you
know, opportunities as well.

00:06:34.499 --> 00:06:36.539
But there was a dialogue that was going on

00:06:36.539 --> 00:06:38.129
that was really troublesome in the sense

00:06:38.129 --> 00:06:40.486
that at that time, the
school board thought,

00:06:40.486 --> 00:06:42.699
"Well, we shouldn't be
teaching Navajo language

00:06:42.699 --> 00:06:44.699
'cause that inhibits
students' ability to read."

00:06:44.699 --> 00:06:46.489
And if you think about
research in general,

00:06:46.489 --> 00:06:48.439
or just like things
that we know to be true,

00:06:48.439 --> 00:06:50.669
when you're bilingual,
trilingual, multilingual,

00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:52.649
those are all major strengths.

00:06:52.649 --> 00:06:57.119
There's a lot of bias to unpack,
a lot of institutionalized,

00:06:57.119 --> 00:07:00.019
I would say deficit mindsets
around the students,

00:07:00.019 --> 00:07:01.659
like, "Are these students
gonna go to college?"

00:07:01.659 --> 00:07:05.819
And even like teachers that I
was becoming the principal of

00:07:05.819 --> 00:07:07.519
would say to me, they'd, you know,

00:07:08.445 --> 00:07:12.339
"The families here don't
necessarily value education,

00:07:12.339 --> 00:07:13.979
and so why would we ask our students

00:07:13.979 --> 00:07:15.039
whether they wanna go to college?"

00:07:15.039 --> 00:07:18.029
And so I tried to like
remain calm and diplomatic,

00:07:18.029 --> 00:07:20.909
at the same time, use data to
prove that that wasn't true,

00:07:20.909 --> 00:07:23.459
which we did by kind of, you know,

00:07:23.459 --> 00:07:25.162
engaging our parents in that conversation,

00:07:25.162 --> 00:07:28.279
and found that like 98% of
the families want their kids

00:07:28.279 --> 00:07:30.209
to go on to a two-to-four-year
college experience.

00:07:30.209 --> 00:07:33.569
So just, that was a really tough situation

00:07:33.569 --> 00:07:34.636
'cause there was a lot of opportunity.

00:07:34.636 --> 00:07:36.459
The school was like fledgling

00:07:36.459 --> 00:07:38.739
and almost went into like a
school improvement process

00:07:38.739 --> 00:07:40.499
that would've put them at danger

00:07:40.499 --> 00:07:42.329
of like being taken over
by the state at that time.

00:07:42.329 --> 00:07:45.269
We were able to turn it around
and really started to think

00:07:45.269 --> 00:07:47.659
about differentiation, more
involvement of student voice,

00:07:47.659 --> 00:07:50.299
all those good things that
people would want their kids

00:07:50.299 --> 00:07:53.459
to have in a school, and we saw progress.

00:07:53.459 --> 00:07:56.469
So as well as like my own personal journey

00:07:56.469 --> 00:07:59.209
around my own identity, growing
up not on the reservation

00:07:59.209 --> 00:08:04.209
but in a city being, you know,
disconnected from my family

00:08:04.259 --> 00:08:06.769
and then having to reconnect
was really a powerful thing

00:08:06.769 --> 00:08:08.799
that I was experiencing.

00:08:08.799 --> 00:08:13.799
But came back home, wanted to
follow up on the idea of NACA

00:08:14.499 --> 00:08:16.719
and really started to engage
the community in that process.

00:08:16.719 --> 00:08:20.289
So I would say it's a lot
of those different things.

00:08:20.289 --> 00:08:23.169
And then also just, you
know, I would say my family

00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:26.799
had a lot of like impact on
me, both like immediately,

00:08:26.799 --> 00:08:29.109
and also some of my grandparents

00:08:29.109 --> 00:08:31.749
who really like forged
the way around education

00:08:31.749 --> 00:08:33.449
within their own communities, too.

00:08:34.939 --> 00:08:36.919
- Wow, that is excellent.

00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:41.549
So that kind of leads us right
into, tell us a little bit

00:08:41.549 --> 00:08:44.689
about the Native American
Community Academy.

00:08:44.689 --> 00:08:47.479
And if we went and visited, what would it,

00:08:47.479 --> 00:08:50.219
what would we see, what
would it look like?

00:08:50.219 --> 00:08:52.999
- So NACA is like synonymous with me

00:08:52.999 --> 00:08:54.899
with the same like word as far as love.

00:08:54.899 --> 00:08:58.169
Like, there is so much
like interconnectedness

00:08:58.169 --> 00:09:00.319
and like relationships among students,

00:09:00.319 --> 00:09:03.099
teachers, and families,
it's truly a community.

00:09:03.099 --> 00:09:05.909
I think of NACA like an
essence of being like something

00:09:05.909 --> 00:09:08.229
that was not in existence
that came into existence.

00:09:08.229 --> 00:09:10.499
It just connected people together in ways

00:09:10.499 --> 00:09:15.419
that grew an entire like web
of support for generations.

00:09:15.419 --> 00:09:17.519
So it's our 16th year.

00:09:17.519 --> 00:09:20.549
You would see students
learning inside and outside,

00:09:20.549 --> 00:09:22.899
so like land-based learning
as well as learning

00:09:22.899 --> 00:09:25.089
like in a traditional, I guess, classroom.

00:09:25.089 --> 00:09:27.049
You would see students
on a college campus,

00:09:27.049 --> 00:09:29.809
and you would also see
early learning taking place

00:09:29.809 --> 00:09:31.799
in a elementary school,

00:09:31.799 --> 00:09:33.859
access to four or five different languages

00:09:33.859 --> 00:09:35.539
that they can take throughout the time

00:09:35.539 --> 00:09:37.829
that they're at the school, Lakota, Zuni,

00:09:37.829 --> 00:09:41.039
Navajo language, Keres, Tiwa, Tewa,

00:09:41.039 --> 00:09:43.139
partnerships with community organizations,

00:09:44.003 --> 00:09:46.059
access to school-based health centers,

00:09:46.059 --> 00:09:48.899
students being able to not
only know like who they are

00:09:48.899 --> 00:09:51.259
and where they come from
but being able to articulate

00:09:51.259 --> 00:09:53.299
a vision they have
that's meaningful to them

00:09:53.299 --> 00:09:54.952
based on their own worldview.

00:09:55.949 --> 00:09:58.089
One of the foundational pieces of NACA

00:09:58.089 --> 00:10:00.029
as we were putting the school together,

00:10:00.029 --> 00:10:03.159
and like what I mean by that
is we literally went out

00:10:03.159 --> 00:10:06.239
and talked to community
members over a period

00:10:06.239 --> 00:10:07.819
of like a year and a half, two years

00:10:07.819 --> 00:10:09.819
about, you know, what
is it that we wanna see

00:10:09.819 --> 00:10:11.069
for Native American students?

00:10:11.069 --> 00:10:13.289
And three things rang true.

00:10:13.289 --> 00:10:15.659
One is that there would be both access

00:10:15.659 --> 00:10:17.649
and like success going to college,

00:10:17.649 --> 00:10:20.009
and that was based on a
lot of people's experience

00:10:20.009 --> 00:10:22.649
about not having that
within their own education

00:10:22.649 --> 00:10:24.529
and/or going to college and not succeeding

00:10:24.529 --> 00:10:28.569
within the first year, for all
sorts of different reasons.

00:10:28.569 --> 00:10:31.049
The second is that the
students would be secure

00:10:31.049 --> 00:10:33.139
in their identity and understanding

00:10:33.139 --> 00:10:34.199
like what is their identity,

00:10:34.199 --> 00:10:36.359
not only as an Indigenous person,

00:10:36.359 --> 00:10:38.269
but maybe somebody who's bicultural

00:10:38.269 --> 00:10:40.519
and somebody who also has like a passion

00:10:40.519 --> 00:10:41.529
in the area of the arts,

00:10:41.529 --> 00:10:44.109
but being able to be
in connection with that

00:10:44.109 --> 00:10:48.299
in a very real way is one major outcome.

00:10:48.299 --> 00:10:50.349
And the last is that
students would be healthy,

00:10:50.349 --> 00:10:53.089
and this is where the NACA Wellness Wheel,

00:10:53.089 --> 00:10:55.349
it's on our website,
anybody can access it,

00:10:55.349 --> 00:10:58.939
is foundational to the
start of the school.

00:10:58.939 --> 00:11:02.009
We talked a lot about like what patterns

00:11:02.009 --> 00:11:04.739
and like aspects of
Native American education

00:11:04.739 --> 00:11:06.329
had not worked in the past,

00:11:06.329 --> 00:11:08.329
and we talked about like some
of the things that we saw

00:11:08.329 --> 00:11:11.799
as far as data and outcomes
for Native students.

00:11:11.799 --> 00:11:14.579
But we wanted to start with
a strengths-based mindset,

00:11:14.579 --> 00:11:16.919
and we selected the Wellness Wheel,

00:11:16.919 --> 00:11:19.162
which is kind of based off of,

00:11:19.999 --> 00:11:23.599
basically a lot of different
tribes think of like people

00:11:23.599 --> 00:11:25.219
as being like whole individuals.

00:11:25.219 --> 00:11:27.099
Like, you're not just coming to school

00:11:27.099 --> 00:11:29.589
to access like one aspect of who you are.

00:11:29.589 --> 00:11:30.929
You bring all of yourself.

00:11:30.929 --> 00:11:33.529
So intellectually, how
are students, you know,

00:11:33.529 --> 00:11:36.729
faring physically, like
your physical wellness,

00:11:36.729 --> 00:11:39.249
as well as your social-emotional wellness

00:11:39.249 --> 00:11:41.209
and your connection to your community

00:11:41.209 --> 00:11:42.899
and overall spirituality,

00:11:42.899 --> 00:11:45.189
and spirituality not being religiosity,

00:11:45.189 --> 00:11:48.209
but realizing like, what are
those things that renew us?

00:11:48.209 --> 00:11:50.529
What are those things that
we can help reflect upon?

00:11:50.529 --> 00:11:53.279
So our students spend a lot of time

00:11:53.279 --> 00:11:54.449
working with the Wellness Wheel

00:11:54.449 --> 00:11:56.442
as well as other courses and classes.

00:11:57.647 --> 00:12:00.709
95% of the student's day is grounded

00:12:00.709 --> 00:12:02.239
in Indigenous perspectives,

00:12:02.239 --> 00:12:03.769
meaning that we're bringing in either,

00:12:03.769 --> 00:12:06.679
whether that's authors or
whether that's like knowledge

00:12:06.679 --> 00:12:09.609
from elders or knowledge
from our content teachers

00:12:09.609 --> 00:12:13.379
in their languages or Indigenous
science and understanding,

00:12:13.379 --> 00:12:15.449
you know, ecological
aspects of different things

00:12:15.449 --> 00:12:16.439
that have taken place.

00:12:16.439 --> 00:12:18.739
But those things are integrated
into how students are,

00:12:18.739 --> 00:12:20.849
how and what students are learning

00:12:20.849 --> 00:12:23.739
and how being really, really
important, I think, too.

00:12:23.739 --> 00:12:25.729
So those are all the
things that you would see.

00:12:25.729 --> 00:12:27.999
I can talk about NACA for several hours,

00:12:27.999 --> 00:12:29.349
but I always feel like,

00:12:29.349 --> 00:12:32.569
like when I think of like
our students are learning

00:12:32.569 --> 00:12:34.829
things that may be, you
know, hard to learn.

00:12:34.829 --> 00:12:37.059
So I think of like kids
in the middle school

00:12:37.059 --> 00:12:39.649
who are understanding
like the role of education

00:12:39.649 --> 00:12:42.169
in Native communities around
boarding schools, right?

00:12:42.169 --> 00:12:44.379
And so they rock, or they experience

00:12:44.379 --> 00:12:45.619
a lot of emotion around that.

00:12:45.619 --> 00:12:47.809
They're doing an
intergenerational interview

00:12:47.809 --> 00:12:49.949
with their grandmother who
went to the boarding school

00:12:49.949 --> 00:12:52.109
who's reflecting back to
them, "I'm glad you're at NACA

00:12:52.109 --> 00:12:54.659
and have access to language
and culture, I didn't,"

00:12:55.539 --> 00:12:57.509
you know, all of those like
really powerful things.

00:12:57.509 --> 00:13:00.699
They take that interview and
transcribe it into an actual,

00:13:00.699 --> 00:13:04.059
you know, written piece, use that piece

00:13:04.059 --> 00:13:06.759
in the like student demonstration

00:13:06.759 --> 00:13:10.829
where they're like presenting
that back to their community.

00:13:10.829 --> 00:13:13.259
And then proactively as an entire class,

00:13:13.259 --> 00:13:16.799
they decide to do something
that's much more positive

00:13:16.799 --> 00:13:18.999
and productive as to like not get caught

00:13:18.999 --> 00:13:22.089
into the negativity or the victimization

00:13:22.089 --> 00:13:25.739
of like our history, but
really being more, I guess,

00:13:25.739 --> 00:13:27.629
like a visionary in the
sense of like they have

00:13:27.629 --> 00:13:29.999
like the ability to
really empower themselves,

00:13:29.999 --> 00:13:31.899
to think about what it is
they wanna see different.

00:13:31.899 --> 00:13:34.466
So a lot of different aspects of that,

00:13:34.466 --> 00:13:36.419
and we really hope that NACA's a place

00:13:36.419 --> 00:13:39.089
where our students can not
only explore their identity

00:13:39.089 --> 00:13:41.269
but act upon it in ways that makes sense,

00:13:41.269 --> 00:13:43.539
like whether that's slam poetry.

00:13:43.539 --> 00:13:47.439
It could be, you know, a
service-oriented project could be,

00:13:47.439 --> 00:13:49.989
you know, anything that
really resonates with them.

00:13:50.979 --> 00:13:53.419
Yeah, so that's just a
little bit about NACA,

00:13:53.419 --> 00:13:55.402
but it's pretty amazing place.

00:13:56.529 --> 00:13:59.209
- Yeah, thanks for sharing all of that.

00:13:59.209 --> 00:14:02.099
It definitely, I have so
many questions. (laughs)

00:14:02.099 --> 00:14:05.619
And I know we wanna talk
about the network piece, too.

00:14:05.619 --> 00:14:09.399
But one of the things that I
know I see in learning research

00:14:09.399 --> 00:14:12.909
is that when students
feel like they belong

00:14:12.909 --> 00:14:14.939
and have that feeling of belongingness,

00:14:14.939 --> 00:14:17.629
we see better learning outcomes as well,

00:14:17.629 --> 00:14:19.749
and it sounds like that that,

00:14:19.749 --> 00:14:23.499
that building in that culture, it then,

00:14:23.499 --> 00:14:25.919
it seems related to that
research of where things are.

00:14:25.919 --> 00:14:29.369
So do you see that relationship
between, for instance,

00:14:29.369 --> 00:14:33.702
the identity and that end
academic achievement as well?

00:14:34.599 --> 00:14:37.759
- Yeah, I would say the
belonging and connectedness,

00:14:37.759 --> 00:14:40.799
of the essence of community
of our school is huge.

00:14:40.799 --> 00:14:43.209
I think of like a story
of one of our students.

00:14:43.209 --> 00:14:45.649
Her mom shared this with me,

00:14:45.649 --> 00:14:49.569
but she came to NACA, and
for the first semester

00:14:49.569 --> 00:14:52.899
at least, six months, was really quiet,

00:14:52.899 --> 00:14:57.319
didn't really share too much,
but was always participating

00:14:57.319 --> 00:14:58.769
and like did what she needed to do,

00:14:58.769 --> 00:15:02.799
and like, you know, was a
really cool kid and just like,

00:15:02.799 --> 00:15:05.679
but didn't really like articulate a lot

00:15:05.679 --> 00:15:07.539
and shares too much with her peers

00:15:07.539 --> 00:15:09.199
or in the classroom setting.

00:15:09.199 --> 00:15:11.479
And then she kinda turned a corner,

00:15:11.479 --> 00:15:13.269
and I remember her mom came
back and said, you know,

00:15:13.269 --> 00:15:17.919
like, "My daughter, the
entire time she was in school

00:15:17.919 --> 00:15:21.029
up until the time she came,
didn't talk in school,

00:15:21.029 --> 00:15:22.869
and now I can't get her to stop talking,"

00:15:22.869 --> 00:15:25.609
like total transformation
in the sense of like now

00:15:25.609 --> 00:15:27.289
she's like leading classroom discussions,

00:15:27.289 --> 00:15:28.779
like, she's leading projects,

00:15:28.779 --> 00:15:30.279
she's like involved in like different

00:15:30.279 --> 00:15:31.629
kind of extracurricular activities.

00:15:31.629 --> 00:15:34.009
And so I feel like that we,

00:15:34.009 --> 00:15:37.249
I know from my own personal experience,

00:15:37.249 --> 00:15:40.809
when you're in a setting
that sees who you are,

00:15:40.809 --> 00:15:42.749
is reflected in what you're learning,

00:15:42.749 --> 00:15:45.579
values who that is, who
you are, and what that is,

00:15:45.579 --> 00:15:49.279
that you open up and you're
able to not have to navigate

00:15:49.279 --> 00:15:52.873
like the worry about like, "How
are people's perceiving me"

00:15:52.873 --> 00:15:55.629
and "Are they going to
make fun of the fact

00:15:55.629 --> 00:15:58.689
that I have to go to a
ceremony for a few days

00:15:58.689 --> 00:16:00.409
with my family and come back,"

00:16:00.409 --> 00:16:02.789
or that there's a song
that's a traditional song

00:16:02.789 --> 00:16:04.109
that we want to share.

00:16:04.109 --> 00:16:07.009
So all of those things are
some of the points of strength,

00:16:07.965 --> 00:16:11.919
and it becomes like just that's
how the school is, right?

00:16:11.919 --> 00:16:14.839
It's not, and this is how our
community has been operating

00:16:14.839 --> 00:16:18.599
since time immemorial,
let alone the fact that,

00:16:18.599 --> 00:16:20.549
you know, kids can be
leading in that space.

00:16:20.549 --> 00:16:22.702
So that's like one small story.

00:16:23.751 --> 00:16:27.689
You know, I do remember the
worst kind of situation of that

00:16:27.689 --> 00:16:28.649
is like when you're in a school setting

00:16:28.649 --> 00:16:31.629
where that's not valued
and it's actually like

00:16:31.629 --> 00:16:34.079
you experience some
sort of microaggression

00:16:34.079 --> 00:16:37.139
and/or like racial, you
know, slur of some sort,

00:16:37.139 --> 00:16:39.879
and then like shuts kids
down really, really quickly.

00:16:39.879 --> 00:16:40.819
So we do see that.

00:16:40.819 --> 00:16:42.289
I would say that we also see

00:16:42.289 --> 00:16:44.189
like some of the students who are involved

00:16:44.189 --> 00:16:45.799
in like learning their language,

00:16:45.799 --> 00:16:50.019
it gives them a bigger
insight into their history

00:16:50.019 --> 00:16:51.679
as well as their identity,

00:16:51.679 --> 00:16:54.059
and that helps set them
up for later success.

00:16:54.059 --> 00:16:56.429
Some of our alumni who
went to different schools

00:16:56.429 --> 00:16:59.379
like ranging from Ivy to
small liberal arts schools,

00:16:59.379 --> 00:17:00.989
community colleges, tribal colleges,

00:17:00.989 --> 00:17:03.046
all kinda report back like,

00:17:03.046 --> 00:17:04.669
"Well, like these are the teachers

00:17:04.669 --> 00:17:06.029
who had the biggest impact on us,"

00:17:06.029 --> 00:17:07.459
which were largely the teachers who had

00:17:07.459 --> 00:17:10.459
most of that cultural
knowledge and connectedness.

00:17:10.459 --> 00:17:12.732
And also they were like inspired.

00:17:12.732 --> 00:17:14.316
Like, "When, we went this," you know,

00:17:14.316 --> 00:17:17.559
"I went to so-and-so school,
I started this like effort

00:17:17.559 --> 00:17:19.889
around Native American students,

00:17:19.889 --> 00:17:21.829
but also all of the
other students of color

00:17:21.829 --> 00:17:23.299
were going through an identity crisis,

00:17:23.299 --> 00:17:24.979
and they were having a really hard time

00:17:24.979 --> 00:17:26.839
and struggling in a different setting,

00:17:26.839 --> 00:17:28.969
and I didn't really go through that."

00:17:28.969 --> 00:17:32.079
Like, "I felt super grounded in who I was.

00:17:32.079 --> 00:17:36.129
A lot of the content we had
already like engaged with,

00:17:36.129 --> 00:17:38.729
and so I felt better
prepared in that sense."

00:17:38.729 --> 00:17:42.119
So I think that definitely
the belonging is a huge piece

00:17:42.119 --> 00:17:43.859
and also understanding your identity.

00:17:43.859 --> 00:17:45.289
And also we're talking about generations

00:17:45.289 --> 00:17:46.929
of folks who've been separated,

00:17:46.929 --> 00:17:48.209
when you think about what boarding schools

00:17:48.209 --> 00:17:50.899
did to our communities
and just not having that

00:17:50.899 --> 00:17:54.279
be the perspective of
education that most students

00:17:54.279 --> 00:17:55.932
across the country engage with.

00:17:57.069 --> 00:17:58.939
- Can you give a little bit
of that history for folks

00:17:58.939 --> 00:18:02.892
that aren't familiar of the
boarding schools and what that,

00:18:04.197 --> 00:18:06.222
the results of that were?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:18:07.079 --> 00:18:11.499
So there's like a quote
that talks about basically

00:18:11.499 --> 00:18:13.419
like "killing the Indian
and saving the man,"

00:18:13.419 --> 00:18:15.859
so this idea that both assimilation

00:18:15.859 --> 00:18:19.369
and kind of direct causation of children

00:18:19.369 --> 00:18:23.199
being removed from their
homes would be more beneficial

00:18:23.199 --> 00:18:26.216
to Native American communities
across the United States.

00:18:26.216 --> 00:18:31.216
And this was like back in the
1800s, and thinking about,

00:18:32.049 --> 00:18:37.049
and also iterations of that
all the way through the time

00:18:37.189 --> 00:18:40.249
that we are now in today.

00:18:40.249 --> 00:18:41.449
So I think of it in a couple ways.

00:18:41.449 --> 00:18:43.239
One, you know, pre-contact,

00:18:43.239 --> 00:18:45.089
there wasn't any kind of interaction

00:18:45.089 --> 00:18:49.649
between Native communities
and Westernized culture,

00:18:49.649 --> 00:18:52.469
and there wasn't even an
institution of education.

00:18:52.469 --> 00:18:54.039
We call that like Indigenous education.

00:18:54.039 --> 00:18:57.659
Then we did have like the treaties

00:18:57.659 --> 00:19:02.329
that were negotiated across the country.

00:19:02.329 --> 00:19:03.486
I think of my tribe, you know,

00:19:03.486 --> 00:19:05.529
the Navajo Nation in particular,

00:19:05.529 --> 00:19:07.659
and one thing that
holds true in that sense

00:19:07.659 --> 00:19:11.409
is that the way I was told was
that our leaders at the time

00:19:11.409 --> 00:19:13.769
were forcibly removed
and all of our people

00:19:13.769 --> 00:19:15.829
from one side of our state to the other

00:19:15.829 --> 00:19:18.089
and imprisoned in an encampment.

00:19:18.089 --> 00:19:20.019
In that encampment, in order to go back

00:19:20.019 --> 00:19:22.579
after 2 1/2 years, they
had to negotiate a treaty.

00:19:22.579 --> 00:19:26.879
Part of that treaty negotiation
was that the Navajo Nation

00:19:26.879 --> 00:19:29.429
would take on one teacher for 25 kids

00:19:29.429 --> 00:19:31.609
that would be provided by
the United States government.

00:19:31.609 --> 00:19:34.849
And the leaders were having
this discussion in Navajo

00:19:34.849 --> 00:19:36.649
with a Spanish interpreter to Spanish

00:19:36.649 --> 00:19:38.969
and then Spanish to English
and English back to Spanish,

00:19:38.969 --> 00:19:40.389
and so it was quite, you know,

00:19:40.389 --> 00:19:42.289
something that was of great concern.

00:19:42.289 --> 00:19:44.039
Otherwise, we would've been removed

00:19:44.039 --> 00:19:46.629
to Oklahoma or potentially Florida.

00:19:46.629 --> 00:19:47.579
So in order to go back

00:19:47.579 --> 00:19:49.339
to where our four sacred mountains are,

00:19:49.339 --> 00:19:51.286
they said, yes, but they asked like,

00:19:51.286 --> 00:19:53.839
"What are the patterns
of this education system?

00:19:53.839 --> 00:19:56.399
How does it work? How does it function?"

00:19:56.399 --> 00:19:58.229
Before that, like learning was seamless

00:19:58.229 --> 00:19:59.749
and within communities

00:19:59.749 --> 00:20:02.489
and also within like the
origins of our seasons.

00:20:02.489 --> 00:20:03.852
So that's one example.

00:20:04.799 --> 00:20:06.089
As we move through, like as you get

00:20:06.089 --> 00:20:07.779
into the '50s and '60s and '70s,

00:20:07.779 --> 00:20:11.639
the resurgence of tribal
colleges as well as other schools

00:20:11.639 --> 00:20:14.619
focused on language and
culture to self-determination,

00:20:14.619 --> 00:20:16.369
and so you started to see schools

00:20:16.369 --> 00:20:19.139
like now Rough Rock Demonstration Schools

00:20:19.139 --> 00:20:21.309
and the Ramah day school

00:20:21.309 --> 00:20:23.859
that were started to
bring language and culture

00:20:23.859 --> 00:20:26.969
and community-based education
back into communities

00:20:26.969 --> 00:20:29.669
versus having students have
to leave their community.

00:20:29.669 --> 00:20:32.359
And now we're in a time where I feel like

00:20:32.359 --> 00:20:34.689
there's such a movement
around Indigenous people

00:20:34.689 --> 00:20:36.539
and also recognizing the importance

00:20:36.539 --> 00:20:38.309
of culturally responsive education

00:20:38.309 --> 00:20:40.419
as well as like what's being taught

00:20:40.419 --> 00:20:42.333
that's really, really important.

00:20:42.333 --> 00:20:44.899
And so it's important to
kids, first and foremost,

00:20:44.899 --> 00:20:47.279
and I think that's what
makes our education better

00:20:47.279 --> 00:20:49.799
and also helps them be more successful

00:20:49.799 --> 00:20:53.499
in being able to do what they need to do

00:20:53.499 --> 00:20:55.589
and knowing who they are and
understanding that history.

00:20:55.589 --> 00:20:57.359
And also all students, I think,

00:20:57.359 --> 00:20:59.022
need to understand that for sure.

00:21:00.281 --> 00:21:01.114
- Yeah!

00:21:01.114 --> 00:21:03.379
- But, yeah.
- And so that leads

00:21:03.379 --> 00:21:05.029
to then the question, the idea,

00:21:05.029 --> 00:21:07.409
all students need to understand this.

00:21:07.409 --> 00:21:10.619
How do we take the things that
you've learned through NACA

00:21:10.619 --> 00:21:13.379
and think about scaling where those are?

00:21:13.379 --> 00:21:16.589
So I know one step was
the NACA Inspired Schools.

00:21:16.589 --> 00:21:18.989
So tell me a little bit about that.

00:21:18.989 --> 00:21:21.259
- Yeah, so like within the
first two years of NACA,

00:21:21.259 --> 00:21:22.466
we were asked several times, like,

00:21:22.466 --> 00:21:24.479
"Can we start a NACA in our community?"

00:21:24.479 --> 00:21:27.026
So when people come to
visit, they're like,

00:21:27.026 --> 00:21:28.459
"Oh my God, this is possible.

00:21:28.459 --> 00:21:29.919
This is what people were writing about

00:21:29.919 --> 00:21:30.979
in the '50s and '60s.

00:21:30.979 --> 00:21:33.999
This is what our elders were saying

00:21:33.999 --> 00:21:36.939
needs to be incorporated
back into school," right?

00:21:36.939 --> 00:21:39.459
So we were like, "Yes,
but it needs to come

00:21:39.459 --> 00:21:40.292
from your community."

00:21:40.292 --> 00:21:41.769
So we're like, "How do we do that?"

00:21:41.769 --> 00:21:44.749
So we put together the NACA
Inspired Schools Network.

00:21:44.749 --> 00:21:46.459
I worked with some folks to think about

00:21:46.459 --> 00:21:47.489
like what that would look like.

00:21:47.489 --> 00:21:48.959
Basically, we support fellows

00:21:48.959 --> 00:21:50.959
who work with their
communities and engage them

00:21:50.959 --> 00:21:52.849
in a similar process as we did it at NACA

00:21:52.849 --> 00:21:56.269
is to come up with their own
mission and vision of a school

00:21:56.269 --> 00:21:58.959
that makes sense for them
around Indigenous education.

00:21:58.959 --> 00:22:03.149
So there's, let's see,
maybe five, six schools

00:22:03.149 --> 00:22:05.539
in New Mexico right now,
some up in South Dakota

00:22:05.539 --> 00:22:07.129
that are coming online,

00:22:07.129 --> 00:22:09.772
Denver as well as another one down in Ute.

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:12.989
And so really it's just
like having a conversation

00:22:12.989 --> 00:22:16.419
about if we were to build
something for our children

00:22:16.419 --> 00:22:20.359
and Native American kids
like wherever that may be,

00:22:20.359 --> 00:22:21.289
what would that look like?

00:22:21.289 --> 00:22:22.569
What would we want to accomplish,

00:22:22.569 --> 00:22:24.039
and how would we go about that?

00:22:24.039 --> 00:22:25.929
So some schools are really heavily focused

00:22:25.929 --> 00:22:27.539
on language and culture.

00:22:27.539 --> 00:22:30.969
Some are really, maybe
a dual-language model.

00:22:30.969 --> 00:22:32.549
Some are focused on immersion.

00:22:32.549 --> 00:22:35.649
All have like the components
of holistic wellness,

00:22:35.649 --> 00:22:37.679
culture, and Indigenous perspectives.

00:22:37.679 --> 00:22:40.809
Some are really, really
focused on land-based learning.

00:22:40.809 --> 00:22:42.619
Others are doing like internships,

00:22:42.619 --> 00:22:44.299
different things like
that, it just depends.

00:22:44.299 --> 00:22:45.516
But that's one way we started to share it.

00:22:45.516 --> 00:22:46.949
The other is we created

00:22:46.949 --> 00:22:48.409
an Indigenous Knowledge Management Hub,

00:22:48.409 --> 00:22:51.549
which is open to anybody to
find like what our teachers

00:22:51.549 --> 00:22:53.279
have created and to contribute

00:22:53.279 --> 00:22:57.382
and/or just utilize those
types of lessons K through 12.

00:22:58.419 --> 00:23:02.759
I feel like so much of this
is like equally important

00:23:02.759 --> 00:23:05.299
to students that are in a traditional,

00:23:05.299 --> 00:23:07.939
maybe comprehensive like,
you know, elementary school,

00:23:07.939 --> 00:23:10.449
middle school, or high
school to understand

00:23:10.449 --> 00:23:12.629
like not only the content
that we have provided,

00:23:12.629 --> 00:23:15.897
but also just being able
to understand the history

00:23:15.897 --> 00:23:18.729
of the United States and like
the role that Native people

00:23:18.729 --> 00:23:20.889
have played and continue to.

00:23:20.889 --> 00:23:23.672
So those are some things that
we've done to help share.

00:23:24.659 --> 00:23:26.253
- Can you say the name of that again,

00:23:26.253 --> 00:23:28.329
that has all the lessons that?

00:23:28.329 --> 00:23:31.829
- Oh, yeah, it's on the NACA
Inspired Schools' website.

00:23:31.829 --> 00:23:34.179
It's called the Indigenous
Knowledge Management Hub,

00:23:34.179 --> 00:23:35.859
which is probably not the most (laughs)-

00:23:35.859 --> 00:23:36.919
- Inspiring name. (laughs)

00:23:36.919 --> 00:23:38.849
- So if anybody out there
has like a suggestion

00:23:38.849 --> 00:23:40.429
on like what to name that,

00:23:40.429 --> 00:23:43.139
feel free to like send that back our away.

00:23:43.139 --> 00:23:45.952
- Excellent, excellent, so you,

00:23:47.259 --> 00:23:49.056
a couple of times had mentioned that,

00:23:49.056 --> 00:23:52.179
and now I've forgotten
what you, what it's called,

00:23:52.179 --> 00:23:55.069
but the land-based teaching.

00:23:55.069 --> 00:23:56.099
- Yeah.
- Can you just tell us

00:23:56.099 --> 00:23:58.549
a little bit about what that
is and what that looks like?

00:23:58.549 --> 00:24:01.569
- Yeah, so when we first started NACA,

00:24:01.569 --> 00:24:04.039
we started with a focus
on experiential education

00:24:04.039 --> 00:24:08.279
as well as like a project
around healthy food

00:24:08.279 --> 00:24:10.479
and Indigenous farming and gardening,

00:24:10.479 --> 00:24:13.769
and so getting kids like that are involved

00:24:13.769 --> 00:24:17.494
in actually working on the
land to do things that,

00:24:17.494 --> 00:24:19.409
to center their learning.

00:24:19.409 --> 00:24:22.669
So that was like the first
kind of iteration of that.

00:24:22.669 --> 00:24:25.259
Now that has grown into a team at NACA

00:24:25.259 --> 00:24:27.889
that started focusing on things

00:24:27.889 --> 00:24:30.099
such as like water and water defenders

00:24:30.099 --> 00:24:31.159
and understanding like what,

00:24:31.159 --> 00:24:34.009
the importance of water
within our communities,

00:24:34.009 --> 00:24:36.979
medicine, and like how
do we identify medicine

00:24:36.979 --> 00:24:40.419
when we're out and about
in different areas,

00:24:40.419 --> 00:24:41.729
whether that's in our home communities

00:24:41.729 --> 00:24:44.539
or within the city or within
the mountains next to us,

00:24:44.539 --> 00:24:48.049
as well as food and understanding
the importance of food

00:24:48.049 --> 00:24:49.739
and healthy food, how food is grown,

00:24:49.739 --> 00:24:54.209
how food systems work, and
how do they exist or not exist

00:24:54.209 --> 00:24:56.139
within our communities as well.

00:24:56.139 --> 00:25:00.119
And then also thinking
about just other ways

00:25:00.119 --> 00:25:02.689
to blend learning around sciences,

00:25:02.689 --> 00:25:03.959
whether that's through literature,

00:25:03.959 --> 00:25:05.219
whether that's through math,

00:25:05.219 --> 00:25:06.999
whether that's through
different content areas

00:25:06.999 --> 00:25:10.029
that teachers can connect to,
like taking their students

00:25:10.029 --> 00:25:12.469
out to the land to be able to do that.

00:25:12.469 --> 00:25:16.289
So, you know, whether that's
a inquiry science project

00:25:16.289 --> 00:25:18.879
or it's a, you know,
taking a group of students

00:25:18.879 --> 00:25:22.779
to harvest pinon for that time

00:25:22.779 --> 00:25:25.549
and understanding why the
significance culturally

00:25:25.549 --> 00:25:29.799
and also the significance of
pinon within the community,

00:25:29.799 --> 00:25:31.319
what that means, and then distribution,

00:25:31.319 --> 00:25:33.029
and like, what does that mean as well?

00:25:33.029 --> 00:25:35.669
So part of that, we
actually are now launching

00:25:35.669 --> 00:25:39.559
Indigenous Farmers Hub, which
is building out a CSA model

00:25:39.559 --> 00:25:42.779
to then like grow food and
be able to provide that food

00:25:42.779 --> 00:25:46.509
back to our students and
families and community at large

00:25:47.489 --> 00:25:49.139
where students are actively engaged

00:25:49.139 --> 00:25:53.769
in understanding how do you
develop a healthy food system

00:25:53.769 --> 00:25:56.109
and what does it mean to grow harvest food

00:25:56.109 --> 00:26:00.789
and create like those opportunities
in Indigenous farming?

00:26:00.789 --> 00:26:03.759
So that's an example,
but something I think

00:26:03.759 --> 00:26:06.649
that also connects our students to land

00:26:06.649 --> 00:26:08.889
and to other people within our community

00:26:08.889 --> 00:26:10.749
who may not be a traditional teacher,

00:26:10.749 --> 00:26:14.199
like in the sense of they
come up through a pipeline,

00:26:14.199 --> 00:26:16.149
that might be through a university,

00:26:16.149 --> 00:26:17.659
but there are those community members

00:26:17.659 --> 00:26:19.219
who hold all this different knowledge

00:26:19.219 --> 00:26:21.419
and like expertise that
they can learn from.

00:26:23.649 --> 00:26:24.482
- Great.

00:26:25.339 --> 00:26:29.889
So if you think about teachers

00:26:29.889 --> 00:26:32.969
in maybe a traditional public
school and where those are

00:26:32.969 --> 00:26:36.049
who want to do and bring more
of this into their classroom,

00:26:36.049 --> 00:26:37.739
what advice do you have for them

00:26:37.739 --> 00:26:41.139
about how to think about
making their classrooms

00:26:41.139 --> 00:26:42.702
more culturally responsive?

00:26:43.949 --> 00:26:46.249
- Yeah, I think knowing
your students and families

00:26:46.249 --> 00:26:48.469
and starting there, and just, I mean,

00:26:48.469 --> 00:26:51.079
and really getting to
build a relationship.

00:26:51.079 --> 00:26:53.359
When I think about being
culturally responsive

00:26:53.359 --> 00:26:55.539
or culturally and linguistically
responsive in education,

00:26:55.539 --> 00:26:56.699
this is like something, you know,

00:26:56.699 --> 00:26:59.249
I spent about 18, 20 months at the state

00:26:59.249 --> 00:27:00.639
thinking about what
are the lessons learned

00:27:00.639 --> 00:27:01.539
that we can share?

00:27:02.419 --> 00:27:04.739
One of them is like really understanding,

00:27:04.739 --> 00:27:06.559
like what's the historical context

00:27:06.559 --> 00:27:10.029
of the community in which
you serve as a teacher,

00:27:10.029 --> 00:27:11.529
whether you have to do that in isolation,

00:27:11.529 --> 00:27:13.799
or you can do that as an entire school.

00:27:13.799 --> 00:27:15.989
Two, engaging your community
and like what it is

00:27:15.989 --> 00:27:19.439
they want to see for their children

00:27:19.439 --> 00:27:20.939
and getting an understanding of that.

00:27:20.939 --> 00:27:24.409
So it helps guide the
processes and protocols

00:27:24.409 --> 00:27:27.729
and like outcomes that you're aiming for.

00:27:27.729 --> 00:27:30.659
I also believe that we have to have

00:27:30.659 --> 00:27:33.479
like open conversations and
courageous conversations,

00:27:33.479 --> 00:27:36.219
and we never, ever wanna set up a student

00:27:36.219 --> 00:27:37.539
to come into a classroom where they feel

00:27:37.539 --> 00:27:39.022
like they don't belong.

00:27:39.899 --> 00:27:42.239
So the sense of belonging
within a classroom setting

00:27:42.239 --> 00:27:44.059
and/or a school setting is something

00:27:44.059 --> 00:27:46.749
that is really, really important.

00:27:46.749 --> 00:27:48.469
And opening up your curriculum

00:27:48.469 --> 00:27:50.139
so that students can explore that

00:27:50.139 --> 00:27:53.459
in a way that's healthy for them and safe,

00:27:53.459 --> 00:27:56.399
and also that brings in
the different dimensions

00:27:56.399 --> 00:27:57.739
of all the students that you serve.

00:27:57.739 --> 00:28:00.209
So one of our parents reflected back to us

00:28:00.209 --> 00:28:02.299
in our first year, like, "When
you said you were gonna serve

00:28:02.299 --> 00:28:06.169
like 62 different tribes
and 13 different ethnicities

00:28:06.169 --> 00:28:07.609
and be able to do that
through your curriculum,

00:28:07.609 --> 00:28:10.476
I thought it was like,
like I wanted to see that.

00:28:10.476 --> 00:28:12.349
And I also thought it
was incredibly ambitious,

00:28:12.349 --> 00:28:13.984
and I had no idea how
you were gonna do that

00:28:13.984 --> 00:28:16.499
with that many different
tribes and perspectives."

00:28:16.499 --> 00:28:19.569
And so talked about like how our teachers

00:28:19.569 --> 00:28:21.559
are able to facilitate like open-ended

00:28:21.559 --> 00:28:24.669
kind of universal questions,
enduring understandings,

00:28:24.669 --> 00:28:26.689
and essential questions
that lended themselves

00:28:26.689 --> 00:28:28.199
to students being able to do that.

00:28:28.199 --> 00:28:30.989
And when he, then in a
focus group came back

00:28:30.989 --> 00:28:33.849
and told us, like, "My son
comes home and he tells me

00:28:33.849 --> 00:28:36.799
not only did he share
about where he's from

00:28:36.799 --> 00:28:39.149
and what he knows about
our family and our culture

00:28:39.149 --> 00:28:41.479
and our background and
like what he was reading,

00:28:41.479 --> 00:28:44.659
he also heard from like
the other, you know,

00:28:44.659 --> 00:28:49.289
15 students in his cohort
about what it was that,

00:28:49.289 --> 00:28:50.759
their family's experience."

00:28:50.759 --> 00:28:52.849
So then it becomes like
just the way to be able

00:28:52.849 --> 00:28:55.152
to share that without
any kinda hesitation.

00:28:56.839 --> 00:28:59.019
And I think that, you know, students now

00:28:59.019 --> 00:29:03.009
like really understand
their own identities

00:29:03.009 --> 00:29:05.109
in ways that I don't believe many of us

00:29:05.109 --> 00:29:07.289
ever had that opportunity in
school to be able to do that.

00:29:07.289 --> 00:29:10.939
So creating real community
and being open-ended

00:29:10.939 --> 00:29:13.129
in like how you're thinking about teaching

00:29:13.129 --> 00:29:14.419
and also letting students lead

00:29:14.419 --> 00:29:16.319
I think is like a good place to start.

00:29:17.369 --> 00:29:21.709
- Yeah, I've heard that
community message from you

00:29:21.709 --> 00:29:25.499
coming through loud and clear
that it really has to be,

00:29:25.499 --> 00:29:28.939
everything is based on
understanding the community

00:29:28.939 --> 00:29:31.959
that you're serving in
and where those are.

00:29:31.959 --> 00:29:34.999
How do you, if you have new
teachers coming into the school,

00:29:34.999 --> 00:29:36.149
how do you prepare them?

00:29:36.149 --> 00:29:38.959
What kinds of training do they go through?

00:29:38.959 --> 00:29:41.369
- Yeah, so a couple things, one, you know,

00:29:41.369 --> 00:29:43.049
as we were growing the school every year,

00:29:43.049 --> 00:29:45.659
we'd have like a new set
of teachers coming in

00:29:45.659 --> 00:29:46.529
to try to do that.

00:29:46.529 --> 00:29:49.859
A lot of it's just modeling,
so like when we started,

00:29:49.859 --> 00:29:53.079
the first day of school
like was very different.

00:29:53.079 --> 00:29:56.199
We started with a song from
one of our Navajo instructors

00:29:56.199 --> 00:29:58.659
as well as a song from one
of our Lakota instructors.

00:29:58.659 --> 00:30:00.819
And it was, we were outside in a circle

00:30:00.819 --> 00:30:03.589
with families and
students at the same time,

00:30:03.589 --> 00:30:07.039
really just acknowledging
like, this is very different,

00:30:07.039 --> 00:30:09.899
but this is like, shouldn't
be different, right?

00:30:09.899 --> 00:30:13.384
It should be the norm, and
it is now the norm, right?

00:30:13.384 --> 00:30:16.049
So just like being able to have,

00:30:16.049 --> 00:30:20.059
and I would always invite our new teachers

00:30:20.059 --> 00:30:21.529
to those types of experiences

00:30:21.529 --> 00:30:23.639
before they come on board
so that they're seeing

00:30:23.639 --> 00:30:26.019
how maybe we close the end of one year out

00:30:26.019 --> 00:30:28.169
and those significant
traditions that we have,

00:30:28.169 --> 00:30:29.469
whether it was like, you know,

00:30:29.469 --> 00:30:31.649
our ceremonies at the end
around student recognition

00:30:31.649 --> 00:30:34.399
and graduation and what those look like,

00:30:34.399 --> 00:30:37.869
whether it was like a
community kind of event

00:30:37.869 --> 00:30:40.029
of some sort, too, whether
it was an annual powwow

00:30:40.029 --> 00:30:42.029
or a feast day that we have in the fall.

00:30:43.309 --> 00:30:45.169
But other than that, it's
like building in the time,

00:30:45.169 --> 00:30:46.180
first of all.

00:30:46.180 --> 00:30:47.849
It's like giving teachers
like three hours a week

00:30:47.849 --> 00:30:50.349
to be able to have time with their teams,

00:30:50.349 --> 00:30:52.449
having a mentor within the school

00:30:52.449 --> 00:30:53.689
that understands the school culture,

00:30:53.689 --> 00:30:56.419
core values, and what does that mean

00:30:56.419 --> 00:30:58.489
when we talk about Indigenous
perspectives in education

00:30:58.489 --> 00:30:59.939
if you're Native or non-Native,

00:30:59.939 --> 00:31:01.929
and you're a teacher at the school,

00:31:01.929 --> 00:31:05.869
as well as we created a
couple different things.

00:31:05.869 --> 00:31:07.099
One is called Growing Educators

00:31:07.099 --> 00:31:08.149
for Native American Communities

00:31:08.149 --> 00:31:10.769
where we co-created
with a community college

00:31:10.769 --> 00:31:14.149
a certification program that
uses the NACA framework,

00:31:14.149 --> 00:31:17.269
a curricular framework to
redesign all of the courses

00:31:17.269 --> 00:31:20.319
that those teachers would
take, and then we took that,

00:31:20.319 --> 00:31:22.999
and that's our professional
development model now.

00:31:22.999 --> 00:31:26.119
So it's grounded on the
kind of notion of k'e,

00:31:26.119 --> 00:31:28.769
which is a word from the Navajo Nation

00:31:28.769 --> 00:31:30.769
which talks about relationships

00:31:30.769 --> 00:31:33.049
and then goes into like the cycle

00:31:33.049 --> 00:31:35.109
that is a Navajo learning cycle,

00:31:35.109 --> 00:31:37.389
which is aligned with the four directions,

00:31:37.389 --> 00:31:40.479
which basically is about
reflection, planning,

00:31:40.479 --> 00:31:42.849
implementation, and then reflection again,

00:31:42.849 --> 00:31:45.229
which is something that has been in place

00:31:45.229 --> 00:31:48.649
within our communities
for a very long time,

00:31:48.649 --> 00:31:51.829
so doing the work by using
like the different aspects

00:31:51.829 --> 00:31:53.989
of what we know to be true.

00:31:53.989 --> 00:31:56.628
The second was the
Indigenous Educators Corps,

00:31:56.628 --> 00:31:58.059
which is one of AmeriCorps,

00:31:58.059 --> 00:32:02.402
which gives, you know, 20-some
young folks, or elders,

00:32:03.344 --> 00:32:04.689
the opportunity to be within schools

00:32:04.689 --> 00:32:07.809
and to serve alongside educators

00:32:07.809 --> 00:32:09.669
in these different schools that we support

00:32:09.669 --> 00:32:12.279
and then hopefully becoming
teachers themselves.

00:32:12.279 --> 00:32:16.529
But I'd say by being connected,
having the time to do that,

00:32:16.529 --> 00:32:18.569
and realizing that there's
not a book or a textbook

00:32:18.569 --> 00:32:20.249
you can pick up that's
going to be able to say,

00:32:20.249 --> 00:32:24.269
like, "This is how you
like indigenize education."

00:32:24.269 --> 00:32:25.759
Eventually maybe somebody will write that.

00:32:25.759 --> 00:32:27.839
One of us should probably
do that at some point,

00:32:27.839 --> 00:32:31.209
but like working collaboratively

00:32:31.209 --> 00:32:34.919
and being able to show what that's like.

00:32:34.919 --> 00:32:37.399
And then always having many resources

00:32:37.399 --> 00:32:40.489
within the individuals who
were around our students

00:32:40.489 --> 00:32:42.519
and families that wanted
to see this happen.

00:32:42.519 --> 00:32:44.269
So that's always been helpful, too.

00:32:46.209 --> 00:32:48.369
- Great, well, I can talk to you all day,

00:32:48.369 --> 00:32:51.019
but we are already at the end of our time.

00:32:51.019 --> 00:32:52.319
- Oh, man!
- Thank you so much

00:32:52.319 --> 00:32:53.509
for sharing your work.

00:32:53.509 --> 00:32:56.359
It is so interesting
and inspiring to hear.

00:32:56.359 --> 00:32:58.429
So thank you for all you're doing.

00:32:58.429 --> 00:33:00.369
- Well, thank you, and
thanks for this opportunity.

00:33:00.369 --> 00:33:02.969
Huge fans of all the work that
you all do in the world, too.

00:33:02.969 --> 00:33:05.857
And so I appreciate
this, and I hope you all

00:33:05.857 --> 00:33:08.952
have a good rest of your month and day.

00:33:10.079 --> 00:33:12.669
- Thank you, and for all those listening,

00:33:12.669 --> 00:33:14.999
we do have another show on Thursday

00:33:14.999 --> 00:33:16.549
with Pedro De Bruyckere.

00:33:16.549 --> 00:33:17.382
He's the author

00:33:17.382 --> 00:33:20.359
of "Urban Myths about
Learning and Education"

00:33:20.359 --> 00:33:23.469
and "More Urban Myths about
Learning and Education,"

00:33:23.469 --> 00:33:25.859
and he will be announcing for
the first time his new book,

00:33:25.859 --> 00:33:29.579
so 12:00 PM Pacific, 3:00
Eastern on Thursday to hear more.

00:33:29.579 --> 00:33:30.979
Thanks, all, for joining us.

00:33:31.911 --> 00:33:34.661
(chimes ringing)

